examples.question_text:
Use the area model to expand $(u+2)(v+5)$, where $u$ and $v$ are positive. The diagram is not drawn to scale.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (4.8,2.4);
\draw[black] (2.8,0)--(2.8,2.4);
\draw[black] (0,0.8)--(4.8,0.8);
\node[above] at (1.4,2.4) {$v$};
\node[above] at (3.8,2.4) {$5$};
\node[left] at (0,1.6) {$u$};
\node[left] at (0,0.4) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Multiply the side lengths of each smaller rectangle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{answerBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (4.8,2.4);
\draw[black] (2.8,0)--(2.8,2.4);
\draw[black] (0,0.8)--(4.8,0.8);
\node[above] at (1.4,2.4) {$v$};
\node[above] at (3.8,2.4) {$5$};
\node[left] at (0,1.6) {$u$};
\node[left] at (0,0.4) {$2$};
\node[text=answerBlue] at (1.4,1.6) {$uv$};
\node[text=answerBlue] at (3.8,1.6) {$5u$};
\node[text=answerBlue] at (1.4,0.4) {$2v$};
\node[text=answerBlue] at (3.8,0.4) {$10$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Add the four areas.
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}(u+2)(v+5)\\&=u(v+5)+2(v+5)\\&=uv+5u+2v+10\end{aligned}$
The terms $5u$ and $2v$ are unlike, so they cannot be collected.
